export const SECTIONS = [
  {
    name: "Domain 1 — Coding Knowledge and Skills",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_NOTE_D1,
    questions: [
      { stem: "A patient with a documented history of breast cancer, status post lumpectomy and completed chemotherapy two years ago, presents for a routine surveillance mammogram with no current evidence of disease. The appropriate primary code for this encounter is most likely:", options: ["An active malignant neoplasm of breast code, since the cancer history is significant", "A personal history of malignant neoplasm of breast code (Z code), since active treatment has been completed and there is no current evidence of disease — the malignancy is no longer active", "An encounter for chemotherapy code, since chemotherapy was previously administered", "No code may be assigned since the cancer is no longer active"], correct: 1, explanation: "Once treatment is completed and there is no evidence of remaining disease, a personal history of malignancy Z code is used rather than an active neoplasm code — a core distinction in the neoplasm coding guidelines between active disease and history of a resolved malignancy." },
      { stem: "A patient undergoes radiation therapy for an active malignancy, with the encounter solely for radiation treatment. Per the neoplasm coding guideline for encounters limited to radiotherapy, the correct sequencing is:", options: ["The malignancy is sequenced first, followed by the encounter-for-radiotherapy code", "The appropriate Z code for encounter for radiotherapy is sequenced first, with the malignancy coded as an additional diagnosis", "Only the malignancy is coded; no Z code is ever used for radiotherapy encounters", "Radiotherapy encounters are never separately coded from surgical treatment encounters"], correct: 1, explanation: "Mirroring the chemotherapy-encounter guideline, when an encounter is solely for radiotherapy, the encounter Z code is sequenced first with the malignancy as an additional diagnosis — a frequently tested parallel structure to the chemotherapy sequencing rule." },
      { stem: "A patient is admitted with an ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) and undergoes emergency PCI with stent placement in the left anterior descending (LAD) artery. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation most likely applies to the stent placement itself?", options: ["Dilation (the vessel lumen is expanded, with a device — the stent — left in place)", "Restriction", "Occlusion", "Bypass"], correct: 0, explanation: "Stent placement to open/expand a narrowed or occluded vessel is coded as Dilation with a device value reflecting the stent left in place — a commonly tested cardiology ICD-10-PCS scenario, distinct from Restriction (narrowing) or Occlusion (closing)." },
      { stem: "Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is used for a coronary artery bypass graft (CABG), reflecting the creation of a new pathway to route blood around a blocked vessel?", options: ["Bypass (altering the route of passage of the contents of a tubular body part)", "Dilation", "Restriction", "Transfer"], correct: 0, explanation: "Bypass is specifically defined as altering the route of passage of the contents of a tubular body part — CABG's defining clinical purpose (rerouting blood flow around a blockage) maps directly to this root operation, distinct from Dilation (which widens the existing lumen rather than creating an alternate route)." },
      { stem: "A patient's documentation states 'NSTEMI' (non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction). Per ICD-10-CM guidance on MI type/timing, which of the following is most relevant to correct code assignment?", options: ["NSTEMI and STEMI are coded identically with no distinction", "ICD-10-CM classifies MI by type (STEMI vs. NSTEMI) and by the specific coronary artery/wall involved when documented, with distinct code categories/subcategories for each — using an unspecified or STEMI code for a documented NSTEMI would be inaccurate", "NSTEMI can never be coded as a principal diagnosis", "Only STEMI is codeable in ICD-10-CM; NSTEMI has no corresponding code"], correct: 1, explanation: "ICD-10-CM distinguishes STEMI from NSTEMI with distinct codes (and further specifies coronary artery/wall site for STEMI when documented) — conflating the two, or defaulting to unspecified when the type is clearly documented, under-codes the specificity actually supported." },
      { stem: "A patient with an acute MI documented as occurring within the 4-week (28-day) timeframe of the initial MI episode, representing a new infarction in a different coronary artery territory, would generally be coded using:", options: ["The same code as the original MI with no distinction", "A code from the subsequent MI category, reflecting a new AMI occurring within 4 weeks of a prior AMI, in addition to the original MI code as appropriate per guidelines", "Only a history-of-MI code, since it's a recurrence", "This scenario cannot be coded under ICD-10-CM"], correct: 1, explanation: "ICD-10-CM has a specific category for a subsequent (new) AMI occurring within 4 weeks of a prior one — a coding nuance distinct from simply re-using the original AMI code or coding it merely as a history." },
      { stem: "Which ICD-10-PCS root operation would most likely apply to a total hip arthroplasty (complete replacement of the hip joint with a prosthetic implant)?", options: ["Replacement", "Insertion", "Supplement", "Repair"], correct: 0, explanation: "Total joint arthroplasty is a classic Replacement scenario — the natural joint (or the diseased portion) is physically taken out and replaced with a prosthetic device that takes its place and function." },
      { stem: "A patient undergoes an open reduction internal fixation (ORIF) of a displaced femur fracture, involving realigning the fracture fragments (reduction) and placing hardware (plates/screws) to stabilize it. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation(s) are most likely involved?", options: ["Reposition (for realigning the bone fragments) and Insertion (for the internal fixation device, if it doesn't itself replace or reinforce a body part)", "Only Excision, since bone is involved", "Only Removal, since hardware is being placed", "Only Supplement, since hardware reinforces the bone"], correct: 0, explanation: "ORIF fracture care typically maps to Reposition (moving the fracture fragments to correct alignment) combined with Insertion of the fixation device (screws/plates/rods) that stabilizes the reduction — a frequently tested multi-root-operation orthopedic ICD-10-PCS scenario." },
      { stem: "A patient with a documented diagnosis of 'chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with acute exacerbation' should be coded using:", options: ["A combination code (where available) or the appropriate COPD code capturing both the chronic condition and the acute exacerbation, since ICD-10-CM classifies COPD with acute exacerbation as a more specific code than COPD alone", "Only the code for acute bronchitis", "Two entirely separate, unrelated codes with no combination logic", "The exacerbation is never separately captured in ICD-10-CM"], correct: 0, explanation: "COPD with acute exacerbation has its own more specific code capturing both elements together — coding only unspecified COPD when an exacerbation is clearly documented under-codes the encounter's actual severity/acuity." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a Hierarchical Condition Category (HCC) as it relates to ICD-10-CM coding and reimbursement?", options: ["HCCs are a completely separate code set from ICD-10-CM used only in dentistry", "HCCs are risk-adjustment categories that CMS maps specific ICD-10-CM diagnosis codes into, used to predict expected healthcare costs (particularly for Medicare Advantage and other risk-adjusted payment models) — meaning coding accuracy and completeness for HCC-relevant diagnoses has direct payment/risk-score implications", "HCCs apply only to inpatient DRG-based reimbursement, never risk-adjusted payment models", "HCCs are determined solely by patient age, unrelated to any diagnosis coding"], correct: 1, explanation: "HCC risk adjustment is a coding-accuracy-sensitive reimbursement mechanism distinct from inpatient DRG methodology — accurately capturing and reporting HCC-relevant chronic conditions (with correct annual recapture, since HCCs generally must be reported each calendar year to remain reflected in a patient's risk score) is a real-world coding knowledge area increasingly tested and practiced." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes why HCC-relevant diagnoses generally must be documented and coded at least once per calendar year (an 'annual recapture' concept) to remain reflected in a patient's risk-adjustment score?", options: ["HCC risk scores are permanent and never need re-documentation once initially captured", "Risk-adjustment models generally reset annually, meaning a chronic HCC-relevant condition not addressed/documented and coded again within the current calendar year may not continue to be reflected in that year's risk score/expected cost calculation, even though the patient still has the condition", "Recapture only applies to conditions that have worsened since the prior year", "HCC recapture is a billing myth with no basis in actual CMS risk-adjustment methodology"], correct: 1, explanation: "Because CMS-HCC and similar models generally operate on an annual basis, a chronic condition must be clinically addressed and coded within each calendar year to continue contributing to that year's risk score — a real, practically significant coding/documentation-completeness concept for value-based and Medicare Advantage-adjacent coding work." },
      { stem: "A patient's record documents type 2 diabetes with diabetic peripheral neuropathy, currently being managed with gabapentin, addressed and reassessed at today's visit. From an HCC-capture standpoard, which best describes correct coding?", options: ["Code only diabetes, unspecified, since it's simpler", "Code the specific combination code for type 2 diabetes with diabetic polyneuropathy, since it is both clinically accurate and, given it is HCC-relevant, must be specifically and accurately captured (not under-coded to unspecified diabetes) to correctly reflect the patient's risk profile for this year", "HCC-relevant diagnoses should never be coded to their most specific level", "Only the neuropathy is coded, with diabetes omitted entirely"], correct: 1, explanation: "Accurate, specific combination coding of diabetes with its diagnosed complication is both the correct ICD-10-CM guideline-driven approach AND clinically/financially significant for HCC capture — under-coding to unspecified diabetes would both misrepresent the clinical picture and understate the risk-adjustment-relevant diagnosis." },
      { stem: "A patient is admitted for a scheduled elective procedure and the physician's expectation, documented at the time of the order, is that the patient will require a hospital stay spanning at least two midnights. This scenario is most directly relevant to which CMS policy concept?", options: ["The Two-Midnight Rule, which generally presumes inpatient admission is appropriate (rather than outpatient/observation status) when the physician expects the patient's care to span at least two midnights", "The HCC risk-adjustment model", "The NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure edit framework", "The HITECH breach notification rule"], correct: 0, explanation: "The Two-Midnight Rule is a CMS medical-necessity/admission-status policy: an expectation (at the time of the admission decision) of a stay crossing two midnights generally supports inpatient status — a Domain 4 regulatory-compliance concept distinct from HCC, NCCI, or HITECH." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the coding/documentation relevance of the Two-Midnight Rule to HIM/coding staff, even though it is primarily a physician admission-decision and utilization-review concept?", options: ["It has no relevance whatsoever to coding or HIM staff", "Coders/HIM staff should understand it as context for why admission status (inpatient vs. observation/outpatient) was assigned, since that status affects which coding/billing rules (UHDDS/DRG vs. outpatient guidelines) apply to the encounter, and inconsistent status-vs-documentation can itself be a compliance/documentation-integrity flag", "The Two-Midnight Rule determines which specific ICD-10-CM code is assigned as principal diagnosis", "The Two-Midnight Rule replaces the need for POA indicator assignment"], correct: 1, explanation: "While admission-status determination is a physician/utilization-review function, HIM/coding staff need to understand it because it determines which entire coding framework (inpatient UHDDS/DRG vs. outpatient) applies — a status inconsistent with clinical documentation is itself a documentation-integrity signal worth recognizing." },
      { stem: "A patient with rheumatoid arthritis is admitted for an unrelated hip fracture repair. The rheumatoid arthritis, well-controlled and not actively addressed during this admission beyond routine home-medication continuation, would generally be:", options: ["Never coded under any circumstance during this admission", "Coded as an additional diagnosis if it meets UHDDS's additional-diagnosis criteria for this stay (e.g., medication continuation/monitoring that is clinically relevant to perioperative management) — routine, unaddressed chronic conditions with no clinical impact on this specific stay would not automatically qualify", "Always coded as the principal diagnosis regardless of the reason for admission", "Coded only if the patient specifically requests it be documented"], correct: 1, explanation: "Whether a stable chronic condition like RA is coded for this admission again depends on the same UHDDS additional-diagnosis test discussed throughout Domain 1 — continued relevant management (e.g., perioperative medication considerations) can qualify it, while a truly unaddressed, non-impactful chronic condition would not." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding when a patient has both an acute and a chronic form of the same condition documented for the same encounter (e.g., acute and chronic systolic heart failure, or acute and chronic kidney disease)?", options: ["Only the chronic form is ever coded", "ICD-10-CM generally provides — and the guidelines direct using — a combination code capturing both the acute and chronic nature together, when such a combination code exists for that condition (as with heart failure), rather than reporting the acute and chronic forms as two separate, unrelated codes", "Only the acute form is ever coded, with the chronic form disregarded entirely", "Acute and chronic combination coding does not exist anywhere in ICD-10-CM"], correct: 1, explanation: "This reinforces the acute-on-chronic combination-code concept already tested for heart failure — a broader ICD-10-CM structural pattern (also seen in some respiratory and other conditions) worth recognizing generally, not memorizing as a one-off heart-failure-specific rule." },
      { stem: "A patient undergoes a diagnostic cardiac catheterization with coronary angiography, revealing significant multi-vessel disease, with no intervention performed during this encounter. Which of the following best reflects correct coding for this encounter?", options: ["The diagnostic catheterization/angiography procedure itself is coded per its own ICD-10-PCS code(s) (reflecting the imaging/measurement procedures actually performed), with no bypass/dilation code assigned since no therapeutic intervention occurred at this encounter", "A CABG code should automatically be assigned since multi-vessel disease was found", "No procedure code is assigned since it was 'only diagnostic'", "The same code used for angioplasty is used regardless of whether intervention occurred"], correct: 0, explanation: "A purely diagnostic catheterization/angiography procedure is coded to reflect what was actually done (imaging/measurement, generally under ICD-10-PCS's Measurement/Imaging sections or Fluoroscopy in the diagnostic radiology-adjacent PCS sections) — not a therapeutic root operation for an intervention that didn't occur at this encounter." },
      { stem: "A patient's discharge diagnosis includes 'protein-calorie malnutrition, severe,' supported by documented BMI, significant weight loss percentage, and a registered dietitian's nutritional assessment consistent with recognized severity criteria. This is an example of:", options: ["A documentation gap requiring an automatic query regardless of the clinical support already present", "An adequately supported, specific diagnosis appropriately codeable to its documented severity level, since sufficient clinical indicators (BMI, weight loss, dietitian assessment) support the severity as documented — consistent with the earlier-established BMI/dietitian-documentation exception discussed for similar scenarios", "A diagnosis that can never be coded because a dietitian, not a physician, performed the nutritional assessment", "Something that must default to the unspecified malnutrition code regardless of supporting documentation"], correct: 1, explanation: "When a specific, severity-graded diagnosis is well-supported by consistent clinical indicators (and the underlying diagnosis itself is provider-documented, with BMI/nutritional assessment data permissibly drawn from a dietitian per the established documentation-source exception), coding to that specific severity is appropriate — not an automatic query trigger or a default to unspecified." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the correct treatment of 'sequela' coding when both the acute phase of a condition AND its late effect are being addressed at the same encounter (e.g., a patient with an old CVA now presenting with a NEW acute stroke)?", options: ["The new acute stroke and any residual sequela of the OLD stroke are both coded as appropriate for the current clinical picture — an old, resolved sequela and a brand-new acute event are not mutually exclusive and are coded according to their own respective guidelines (sequela coding for the old residual, acute stroke coding for the new event)", "Only the new acute event may ever be coded; old sequelae are always dropped once a new acute event occurs", "The two must always be combined into a single code", "Sequela codes automatically expire after one year and can never be used again after that"], correct: 0, explanation: "A new acute event and a prior sequela are clinically and codably distinct — both may appropriately be coded together when both are clinically relevant to the current encounter, following their own respective guideline logic (acute condition coding for the new event, sequela coding for the residual of the old one)." },
      { stem: "A patient is admitted with acute pancreatitis and, per the discharge summary, the etiology is specifically and clearly documented as 'gallstone pancreatitis' (biliary etiology). Correct coding requires:", options: ["Using the unspecified acute pancreatitis code, since etiology documentation doesn't affect code selection", "Using the ICD-10-CM code specifically capturing biliary/gallstone-related acute pancreatitis, if the classification provides a more specific option, rather than defaulting to an unspecified or idiopathic code when a clear etiology is actually documented", "Coding it as chronic pancreatitis instead, since gallstones are a chronic risk factor", "Querying is always mandatory even when etiology is already clearly documented"], correct: 1, explanation: "When a specific etiology is clearly documented (unlike the earlier example of no determinable cause despite workup), coding should reflect that specificity to the extent the classification allows — this contrasts directly with the earlier idiopathic-pancreatitis scenario, illustrating that code specificity should track what's actually documented in each case." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the correct ICD-10-PCS coding approach for a laparoscopic appendectomy with no complications?", options: ["Root operation Resection (the entire appendix is removed), approach percutaneous endoscopic (laparoscopic), body part appendix", "Root operation Excision, approach open, body part appendix", "Root operation Extraction, approach external, body part appendix", "Root operation Destruction, approach via natural orifice"], correct: 0, explanation: "A routine appendectomy removes the entire appendix (Resection, not Excision, since none of the appendix remains), performed via a percutaneous endoscopic (laparoscopic) approach — matching the correct root operation/approach/body part combination for this common general-surgery scenario." },
      { stem: "A patient with a below-knee amputation for peripheral vascular disease with gangrene would most likely be coded using which ICD-10-PCS root operation for the amputation procedure itself?", options: ["Detachment (cutting off all or a portion of the upper or lower extremities)", "Excision", "Resection", "Release"], correct: 0, explanation: "Detachment is the specific root operation reserved for cutting off all or a part of an upper or lower extremity — distinct from Excision/Resection, which apply to non-extremity body parts generally, making Detachment a commonly tested extremity-specific root operation." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best reflects correct sequencing when a patient is admitted with both an acute condition requiring immediate treatment (e.g., acute cholecystitis) and undergoes a procedure addressing that same condition (cholecystectomy) during the stay?", options: ["The acute condition (cholecystitis) is coded as principal diagnosis (assuming it meets the UHDDS definition for this admission), with the cholecystectomy coded as the principal procedure, since UHDDS principal diagnosis selection is based on the reason for admission, not automatically on whether a procedure was performed", "The procedure code always determines/overrides the principal diagnosis selection", "Only the procedure is coded; the underlying diagnosis is never separately coded when surgery is performed for it", "The two must always be combined into a single procedure-plus-diagnosis code"], correct: 0, explanation: "Principal diagnosis selection remains governed by UHDDS (the reason for admission), independent of whether/which procedure was performed to treat that condition — the procedure and diagnosis are coded and sequenced according to their own respective rules, not collapsed into one code or having the procedure dictate diagnosis sequencing." },
      { stem: "A patient with end-stage renal disease (ESRD) on chronic hemodialysis is admitted for an unrelated elective procedure. Which of the following best reflects correct coding of the ESRD/dialysis status for this admission?", options: ["ESRD is coded as an additional diagnosis when it meets the UHDDS additional-diagnosis criteria for this stay (e.g., ongoing dialysis management during the admission), reflecting its ongoing clinical significance, and the specific CKD stage 5/ESRD code (not an unspecified CKD code) should be used when documented", "ESRD is never coded during an unrelated admission", "ESRD is always coded as the principal diagnosis regardless of the reason for admission", "Dialysis status has no bearing on which CKD-stage code is selected"], correct: 0, explanation: "ESRD/dialysis-dependent status is a chronic condition that, when it meets the additional-diagnosis criteria (e.g., ongoing dialysis needs managed during the stay), is appropriately coded to its specific stage — this also ties to the earlier CKD staging concept and HCC risk-adjustment relevance, since ESRD is a significant HCC category." },
      { stem: "A patient with a documented BMI of 42 and a physician-documented diagnosis of 'morbid (severe) obesity' undergoes a laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation most likely applies to removal of a portion of the stomach to reduce its size?", options: ["Excision (removing a portion of the stomach, not all of it)", "Resection (since it is bariatric surgery)", "Restriction only, with no tissue removed", "Destruction, since tissue is eliminated"], correct: 0, explanation: "A sleeve gastrectomy removes a PORTION of the stomach (not the entire organ), mapping to Excision rather than Resection (entire body part) — a commonly tested bariatric-surgery root-operation distinction." },
      { stem: "A patient undergoes a laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding procedure, in which a band is placed around the upper stomach to narrow its diameter without removing any tissue. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation applies?", options: ["Restriction (altering the diameter of a tubular body part without taking out or replacing any of it)", "Excision", "Resection", "Supplement"], correct: 0, explanation: "Gastric banding narrows the stomach's diameter using a device, without removing or replacing any stomach tissue — the defining scenario for Restriction, distinct from the tissue-removing Excision used for sleeve gastrectomy." },
      { stem: "A patient with a documented history of deep vein thrombosis (DVT), now on long-term anticoagulation with no current active clot, presents for an unrelated visit. The DVT history is most appropriately coded, if relevant to current care, using:", options: ["An active DVT code, since anticoagulation is ongoing", "A personal history of DVT/pulmonary embolism Z code, since the acute clot has resolved and the patient is in a maintenance/prevention phase of care — the ongoing anticoagulation itself may also be separately reflected (e.g., via a long-term drug therapy Z code) as clinically relevant", "No code at all, since the condition has resolved", "The same code as an active DVT regardless of resolution status"], correct: 1, explanation: "A resolved acute VTE event on maintenance anticoagulation is coded with history/long-term-therapy Z codes rather than an active-clot code — mirroring the same active-vs-history distinction pattern already established for other resolved acute conditions." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the coding treatment of a patient's 'do not resuscitate' (DNR) status or other advance directive documentation, from an ICD-10-CM coding standpoint?", options: ["DNR status is always coded as a principal diagnosis", "ICD-10-CM includes a specific Z code category for documenting the presence of DNR status/advance directives when clinically relevant to the encounter, though this Z code reflects a status/circumstance rather than a diagnosis driving clinical treatment decisions", "DNR status can never be coded under any circumstance", "DNR status is coded using the same codes as a terminal illness diagnosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "A specific Z code exists to capture DNR status/presence of advance directives as a relevant circumstance of care — distinct from, and not a substitute for, coding the patient's actual underlying diagnoses." },
      { stem: "A patient with chronic atrial fibrillation on long-term anticoagulation is admitted for an unrelated surgical procedure requiring temporary anticoagulation interruption ('bridging'). Which of the following diagnoses would most likely be coded as an additional diagnosis, if clinically addressed/managed during this stay?", options: ["The atrial fibrillation itself, since its management (anticoagulation bridging) is being actively addressed during this admission, meeting UHDDS's clinical-management criteria for an additional diagnosis", "Nothing related to atrial fibrillation should ever be coded during an unrelated surgical admission", "Only the surgical diagnosis may be coded; all chronic conditions are automatically excluded", "Atrial fibrillation can only be coded if it is the principal diagnosis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Active perioperative management of a chronic condition (here, anticoagulation bridging for AFib) meets the UHDDS clinical-management criterion for a reportable additional diagnosis — reinforcing the general 'actively managed during this stay' test applied throughout Domain 1." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the ICD-10-PCS approach value 'percutaneous' as distinguished from 'percutaneous endoscopic'?", options: ["They are identical and interchangeable terms", "Percutaneous involves entry by puncture or minor incision to reach the site of the procedure (without visualization), while percutaneous endoscopic involves the same type of entry but with the additional use of a scope/camera to visualize the procedure site", "Percutaneous endoscopic never involves any incision or puncture at all", "Percutaneous applies only to cardiac procedures"], correct: 1, explanation: "The key distinction is visualization: percutaneous is a puncture/minor incision WITHOUT a scope, while percutaneous endoscopic adds visualization via a scope through that same type of entry — a foundational ICD-10-PCS approach-value distinction." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the ICD-10-PCS approach value 'via natural or artificial opening'?", options: ["Entry into the body occurs through a natural orifice (e.g., mouth, vagina) or a pre-existing artificial opening (e.g., an established stoma), without cutting through skin/mucous membrane", "This approach always requires an incision through the skin", "This approach applies only to cardiac catheterization procedures", "This approach is identical to the open approach"], correct: 0, explanation: "This approach category reflects entry via an existing natural or artificial opening, without any cutting — distinct from Open (cutting through skin/tissue to expose the site) and from percutaneous approaches (puncture/minor incision)." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes correct coding when a diagnostic procedure is performed via one approach, and later in the SAME operative session a related therapeutic procedure is performed via a DIFFERENT approach (e.g., diagnostic laparoscopy followed by conversion to open therapeutic surgery)?", options: ["Both procedures are always coded using only the initial approach, regardless of what was actually completed for the therapeutic portion", "Coding should reflect the approach actually used for each distinct procedure performed/completed, consistent with the general principle that a converted or changed approach is coded as what was actually done — the same conversion logic already discussed applies whether the change happens mid-single-procedure or between a diagnostic and a subsequent therapeutic procedure", "Only the therapeutic procedure may ever be coded, with the diagnostic portion never separately considered", "Approach is never a coding consideration when multiple procedures occur in the same session"], correct: 1, explanation: "This generalizes the earlier conversion-coding principle: each distinct procedure's code should reflect the approach ACTUALLY used to complete that specific procedure, not an approach abandoned partway through or one used for a different procedure in the same session." },
      { stem: "A patient undergoes a procedure described as 'taking out or eliminating solid matter from a body part by scraping,' commonly performed on the endometrium (e.g., D&C). Which ICD-10-PCS root operation applies?", options: ["Extraction", "Excision", "Fragmentation", "Drainage"], correct: 0, explanation: "Extraction covers pulling/stripping/scraping out solid matter using force to separate it — a D&C (dilation and curettage) is a classic Extraction example, distinct from Excision (cutting, not scraping/pulling) of a defined anatomic portion." },
      { stem: "A patient's ICD-10-CM coding for a pressure ulcer requires documentation of which of the following, per the pressure ulcer coding guidelines?", options: ["Only the anatomic site, with stage never being a coding factor", "Both the anatomic site AND the stage (unstageable, stage 1 through 4, or deep tissue pressure injury) of the pressure ulcer, since ICD-10-CM's pressure ulcer codes are structured by site-and-stage combination", "Only whether the ulcer is present, with no further detail ever coded", "Pressure ulcer coding requires a surgical biopsy before any code may be assigned"], correct: 1, explanation: "Pressure ulcer ICD-10-CM codes are structured around both site and stage — omitting stage (defaulting to unspecified when a stage is actually documented, e.g., by wound care nursing per the same documentation-source exception discussed earlier) under-codes the encounter." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes correct handling when a pressure ulcer progresses from a lower stage (e.g., stage 2) to a higher stage (e.g., stage 3) during the same admission?", options: ["Only the highest stage ever reached during the stay is coded, since ICD-10-CM guidance directs coding the highest stage documented for that site during the episode of care when progression is documented", "Only the admission stage is ever coded, regardless of progression", "Both stages must be coded as two entirely separate, unrelated ulcers", "Pressure ulcer staging progression can never be coded under any circumstance"], correct: 0, explanation: "Guidance directs coding to the highest stage documented for a given pressure ulcer site during the stay when progression occurs — not simply the admission or discharge stage in isolation if a higher stage was documented at some point during the episode." },
      { stem: "A patient with sickle cell disease presents in acute vaso-occlusive crisis. Correct ICD-10-CM coding requires:", options: ["A combination code reflecting the specific sickle cell disease type WITH the crisis (e.g., 'with crisis' or 'with vaso-occlusive pain'), when the classification provides that combination, rather than coding sickle cell disease and the crisis as unrelated codes", "Only a generic 'pain, unspecified' code, with sickle cell disease never coded during a crisis", "Sickle cell crisis is never separately identified in ICD-10-CM from the base disease code", "Coding requires genetic testing confirmation before any sickle cell code may be used, regardless of a prior established diagnosis"], correct: 0, explanation: "ICD-10-CM's sickle cell disease codes include combination options capturing the disease WITH a crisis when documented — reflecting the same combination-code principle already seen with diabetes and heart failure, applied to a hematologic condition." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the general ICD-10-CM guideline concerning 'multiple coding for a single condition' (as opposed to a single combination code)?", options: ["Multiple codes are never used for a single underlying condition under any circumstance", "Some conditions require reporting more than one code to fully capture the condition (e.g., an underlying etiology code plus a manifestation code, per 'code first'/'use additional code' conventions already discussed), representing a deliberate classification design choice rather than a coding error", "Multiple coding always indicates a coder mistake requiring correction", "Multiple coding applies only to procedure codes, never diagnosis codes"], correct: 1, explanation: "Certain conditions are deliberately classified using more than one code (etiology/manifestation pairs, combination-code-plus-additional-detail scenarios) — recognizing when this is the CORRECT approach (versus true unbundling/inappropriate duplication) is a foundational ICD-10-CM structural concept." },
      { stem: "A patient with documented 'acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)' following major trauma would generally be coded:", options: ["Using the specific ARDS code, with the underlying cause/associated trauma also coded per applicable guidelines, rather than coding only a generic respiratory failure code when ARDS is specifically documented", "Using only a generic pneumonia code regardless of the ARDS diagnosis", "ARDS is never separately coded from its underlying cause", "Only the trauma injury codes are used, with no respiratory code at all"], correct: 0, explanation: "ARDS has its own specific ICD-10-CM code, distinct from generic respiratory failure — when a physician specifically documents ARDS (a distinct, more severe clinical entity), coding to that specificity (alongside the underlying cause) is expected rather than defaulting to a more generic respiratory code." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the treatment of 'debridement' coding in the ICD-10-PCS system for an inpatient excisional debridement of a pressure ulcer down to and including muscle tissue?", options: ["Excisional debridement involving muscle would generally be coded using the Excision root operation with the muscle body part value (reflecting the deepest tissue layer actually debrided), consistent with the general ICD-10-PCS principle of coding to the deepest/most extensive tissue plane involved", "Debridement is never separately coded in ICD-10-PCS regardless of depth", "All debridements are coded identically regardless of depth using a single universal code", "Only non-excisional (mechanical) debridement can ever be coded in ICD-10-PCS"], correct: 0, explanation: "ICD-10-PCS excisional debridement coding depends on the deepest tissue layer actually debrided (skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscle, bone, etc.) — mirroring the CPT-side debridement depth-dependency concept discussed for the outpatient/physician setting, applied here to the inpatient ICD-10-PCS context." },
      { stem: "A patient's health record documents 'malignant hypertension' as a diagnosis. Correct ICD-10-CM treatment of this term is best described as:", options: ["ICD-10-CM classifies hypertension primarily by type (e.g., essential, secondary, with heart/kidney involvement) rather than by the older severity-descriptor terms like 'malignant/benign' hypertension, which no longer have distinct code assignments the way they did in some older classification systems — a coder should code to the hypertension type actually reflected in current documentation/classification structure", "Malignant hypertension is coded identically to a malignant neoplasm", "Malignant hypertension can never be coded under ICD-10-CM", "Only benign hypertension exists as a codeable concept in ICD-10-CM"], correct: 0, explanation: "This tests awareness that ICD-10-CM's hypertension classification structure emphasizes type (essential, with heart/kidney involvement, secondary) rather than preserving older benign/malignant severity distinctions from prior classification editions — a classification-structure nuance worth recognizing rather than assuming older terminology maps directly." },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Domain 2 — Coding Documentation",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_NOTE_D2,
    questions: [
      { stem: "A cardiology consult note documents 'EF 35%' without stating the type of heart failure (systolic vs. diastolic) or whether it is acute, chronic, or acute-on-chronic. The echocardiogram report in the same chart clearly states 'reduced ejection fraction consistent with systolic dysfunction.' The correct documentation-review approach is:", options: ["Ignore the echo report and code only unspecified heart failure, since the physician note alone doesn't specify type", "Recognize that clinical data elsewhere in the record (the echo report) may support greater specificity, and consider whether the physician's own clinical documentation (not just a coder's independent interpretation of a raw test result) supports coding to that specificity, querying if it remains genuinely unclear whether the physician agrees with connecting the echo finding to an active heart failure diagnosis", "Code directly from the echo report's technical findings as if it were the physician's own diagnostic statement, without regard to whether the physician's own note supports that connection", "Never consider ancillary test reports when coding diagnoses"], correct: 1, explanation: "This is a nuanced area: coders should look at the whole record for supporting specificity, but generally should not independently diagnose from a raw test result alone without the physician's own clinical documentation connecting that finding to a diagnosis — genuine ambiguity about whether the physician agrees with linking the test finding to a specific diagnosis is a legitimate query trigger, not something to resolve by coder inference alone." },
      { stem: "Which best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity practice when HCC-relevant conditions (e.g., chronic conditions affecting risk adjustment) are involved?", options: ["Assume every condition ever listed on a problem list qualifies for HCC capture regardless of whether it was addressed at the current encounter", "Verify that HCC-relevant diagnoses are genuinely addressed/evaluated at the specific encounter (consistent with the encounter's own documentation, not simply copied from a prior year's problem list) before including them in that encounter's coding, since accurately reflecting what was actually assessed matters both clinically and for risk-adjustment integrity", "HCC-relevant diagnoses require no documentation verification since they are pre-approved by the payer", "Only physicians, never any HIM/coding staff, should ever review HCC-relevant documentation"], correct: 1, explanation: "A recognized real-world documentation-integrity risk in risk-adjustment coding is 'diagnosis carry-forward' without actual current-encounter clinical support — verifying genuine current-encounter documentation (not just historical problem-list copying) is a coding-documentation-integrity practice directly relevant to HCC accuracy." },
      { stem: "A patient's admission order states 'admit for inpatient care, expected LOS 2+ midnights' but the discharge summary shows the patient was actually discharged after only 18 hours with no complications preventing earlier discharge. This scenario illustrates:", options: ["A documentation/status consistency question worth reviewing — since the actual course of the stay differed substantially from the physician's initial expectation, understanding why (an unexpectedly rapid recovery, or a potential status-determination issue) is relevant to accurate status/UHDDS reporting, though a good-faith physician expectation at the time of the order, even if the stay didn't ultimately reach two midnights, does not automatically make the inpatient order wrong", "This scenario always means the inpatient order was fraudulent and must be reported immediately without any further review", "Discharge timing has no bearing on documentation review under any circumstance", "The discrepancy must always be resolved by changing the diagnosis code, not the admission status"], correct: 0, explanation: "A stay that doesn't reach the expected two midnights doesn't automatically invalidate a good-faith physician expectation documented at the time of the admission decision — but a pattern or an unexplained large discrepancy is still worth understanding/reviewing as part of documentation-integrity practice, not simply ignored or over-reacted to as presumed fraud." },
      { stem: "Which best reflects a sound documentation-integrity practice when abstracting HL7/FHIR-sourced structured data (e.g., problem list entries received electronically from another organization via interoperability exchange) directly into a patient's chart for potential coding use?", options: ["Structured data received electronically from another organization is always 100% accurate and requires no clinical verification before being relied upon for coding", "Externally-sourced structured data (even when received via a standardized interoperability format) should be reviewed for clinical currency/accuracy and ideally corroborated by the receiving organization's own clinical documentation before being relied upon for that organization's own coding, since data accuracy at the source doesn't guarantee continued clinical relevance for the current encounter", "HL7/FHIR-formatted data can never be used for coding under any circumstance", "Only manually-typed documentation may ever be used for coding; structured/interoperable data is categorically excluded"], correct: 1, explanation: "Interoperability standards (HL7/FHIR) solve the technical exchange problem, not the clinical-currency/accuracy verification problem — externally sourced structured data still needs the receiving organization's own clinical corroboration before being relied upon for that encounter's coding, a nuance connecting Domain 2 documentation integrity to Domain 5 interoperability concepts." },
      { stem: "A discharge summary for a patient with a hip fracture repair states 'patient tolerated procedure well' with no documentation anywhere in the record of the specific ICD-10-PCS-relevant details (approach, device used, whether reduction was open or closed). This is best characterized as:", options: ["Sufficient documentation, since the discharge summary confirms the patient did well", "A significant documentation gap for procedure coding purposes — the discharge summary's general outcome statement doesn't substitute for the operative report's technical details needed to assign an accurate ICD-10-PCS code, and the operative report (not the discharge summary) should be the primary source reviewed for this information", "Proof that no procedure was actually performed", "A gap that can only be resolved by discharging and readmitting the patient"], correct: 1, explanation: "General outcome language in a discharge summary is not a substitute for the specific technical documentation (in the operative report) needed for accurate ICD-10-PCS coding — the correct response is reviewing the actual operative report, not treating the discharge summary's general statement as sufficient." },
      { stem: "A physician's echocardiogram order and the cardiology report both use the abbreviation 'CHF' with no further specification, while a separate nursing assessment on the same date documents 'bilateral lower extremity edema, crackles bilaterally, JVD' — clinical findings consistent with, but not explicitly labeled by nursing as, heart failure. The correct documentation-review approach is:", options: ["Code from the nursing findings alone as if they were an independent physician diagnosis of a specific heart failure type", "Recognize that nursing clinical findings can provide useful supporting CONTEXT but do not themselves establish a physician diagnosis or its specific type — review the physician's own documentation further for type specificity, and query if genuinely needed, rather than deriving a specific diagnosis type from nursing assessment findings alone", "Ignore the nursing findings entirely as irrelevant to coding", "Code the most severe possible heart failure type based on the symptom pattern"], correct: 1, explanation: "Nursing clinical findings are valuable context but don't themselves establish a specific physician diagnosis — the coder should look for or query the physician's own diagnostic determination rather than deriving a specific diagnosis type from nursing assessment data alone." },
      { stem: "A patient's chart contains an outdated allergy list (not updated in over a year) inconsistent with a more recent inpatient nursing reconciliation showing a new documented drug allergy. Which best reflects the appropriate documentation-integrity handling of this discrepancy?", options: ["Ignore the discrepancy since allergy lists are rarely accurate anyway", "Recognize this as a patient-safety-relevant documentation-reconciliation issue that should be resolved/updated in the record (the more recent, verified reconciliation should generally govern going forward), even though allergy-list accuracy is not itself typically an ICD-10-CM coding element", "Code a new diagnosis reflecting the allergy discrepancy itself", "Automatically delete the older allergy list without any reconciliation process"], correct: 1, explanation: "While allergy list accuracy isn't itself a coded diagnosis element, recognizing and appropriately flagging/reconciling a patient-safety-relevant documentation inconsistency is a legitimate HIM/documentation-integrity awareness point, distinct from a coding-specific action." },
      { stem: "Which best reflects a sound documentation-integrity practice regarding HCC-relevant chronic conditions that a patient's PRIMARY care record shows as active, but that are not mentioned at all in a SPECIALIST's note for an unrelated encounter?", options: ["Every chronic condition from the primary care record must always be copied into every specialist encounter's coding regardless of relevance", "Recognize that HCC/chronic-condition coding for a SPECIFIC encounter should reflect what was actually addressed/relevant to THAT encounter, not automatically import every condition from a different provider's separate record — cross-encounter 'diagnosis carry-forward' without current-encounter relevance is a recognized documentation-integrity and compliance risk", "Only the specialist's own historical conditions may ever be coded, never anything from primary care", "Specialist encounters can never have any HCC-relevant diagnoses coded under any circumstance"], correct: 1, explanation: "This reinforces the current-encounter-relevance principle already established for HCC coding — a condition being active/documented in a DIFFERENT provider's record doesn't automatically make it reportable for an unrelated encounter that didn't actually address it." },
      { stem: "A physician's progress note references 'per outside records, patient has ESRD on dialysis' without further verification/corroboration within the current facility's own clinical assessment. From a documentation-integrity standpoint for coding this specific admission, this is best handled by:", options: ["Automatically coding ESRD without any further consideration, since it's mentioned in a note", "Recognizing that a physician's own note referencing and adopting outside information (even if originally from another source) can support coding IF the physician's documentation reflects their own clinical acknowledgment/adoption of that information as part of the current assessment — the key is whether the treating physician has incorporated it into their own clinical picture, not merely whether it originated elsewhere", "Never coding any condition that was ever referenced as coming from an outside source", "Requiring an outside facility's dialysis records to be physically present in the chart before ESRD may ever be coded"], correct: 1, explanation: "A treating physician's own documentation that incorporates and relies on outside information as part of their current clinical assessment can support coding — the critical factor is the treating physician's own clinical acknowledgment, not simply whether the underlying fact originated at another facility." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the documentation standard needed to support a specific ICD-10-PCS device value distinguishing a 'drug-eluting' from a 'non-drug-eluting' intraluminal device (e.g., stent) in a cardiac catheterization procedure?", options: ["The operative/procedure report or device/implant log must specifically document the stent type used, since this device characteristic directly affects code/device-value selection and cannot be assumed by default", "Device type is never a coding consideration for stent placement procedures", "All stents are assumed to be drug-eluting by default unless documented otherwise", "Device value specificity is optional and coder discretion governs regardless of documentation"], correct: 0, explanation: "This reinforces the earlier device-specificity documentation principle (also discussed for CCS Paper 2) applied specifically to cardiac stents — explicit documentation of the actual device type is required, not a default assumption in either direction." },
      { stem: "A hospital's coding staff notice that operative reports for a specific surgeon are consistently missing documentation of approach (open vs. laparoscopic vs. robotic-assisted) despite the procedure clearly having been performed using minimally invasive technique per the surgery schedule/equipment used. The appropriate response is best described as:", options: ["Assume laparoscopic approach by default for that surgeon going forward without further verification each time", "Recognize this as a recurring, surgeon-specific documentation gap warranting both individual case-level query/verification and broader physician documentation education/feedback (a systemic pattern, not just a one-off missing detail), rather than either guessing a default approach or repeatedly querying without addressing the root cause", "Ignore the pattern since surgery schedules aren't official clinical documentation", "Refuse to code any of that surgeon's cases indefinitely"], correct: 1, explanation: "A recurring, surgeon-specific documentation gap is both an individual-case query opportunity AND a signal for broader physician documentation education — recognizing and addressing the systemic pattern (not just guessing a default or repeatedly querying without addressing root cause) reflects mature documentation-integrity practice." },
      { stem: "A patient's admission H&P documents 'possible sepsis, rule out UTI vs. pneumonia' with subsequent notes over the stay never definitively resolving which condition (if either) was the actual source, though antibiotics were continued and the patient improved. At discharge, the summary states only 'infection, resolved.' From a documentation-integrity standpoint, this represents:", options: ["Adequate documentation requiring no further review, since 'infection, resolved' is sufficient for any coding purpose", "A specificity gap — the discharge summary's vague closing characterization doesn't resolve which specific diagnosis (if a specific source was ever actually confirmed) should be coded, warranting further review of the full course of the stay and/or a query to clarify the physician's final determination", "Grounds to automatically code both UTI and pneumonia as confirmed diagnoses", "Grounds to code only 'sepsis' as the final diagnosis regardless of what the record shows"], correct: 1, explanation: "A vague discharge characterization following an ambiguous workup doesn't by itself resolve the specific diagnosis — this calls for reviewing the full stay's documentation and/or a compliant query, not assuming either diagnosis or defaulting to the broadest term used earlier in the stay." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best reflects a documentation-integrity best practice when a patient is transferred between units (e.g., ICU to a medical floor) during a single hospital stay, with respect to diagnosis and status continuity?", options: ["Each unit's documentation is treated as an entirely separate, unrelated encounter for coding purposes", "The patient's diagnoses/status should be tracked consistently across the full continuum of the single inpatient stay, with transfer-related notes reviewed to ensure no relevant diagnosis, complication, or status change occurring around the transfer point is lost between documentation sources", "Only the ICU documentation is ever used for coding, regardless of what happens after transfer", "Unit transfers automatically trigger a new principal diagnosis determination"], correct: 1, explanation: "A single inpatient stay's coding should reflect the whole continuum of care — reviewing documentation across unit transfers (not just the final unit's notes) helps ensure nothing clinically significant occurring around a transfer point is missed." },
      { stem: "A hospital's EHR autocorrect/autotext feature inserts a standard normal-exam template phrase into a note, but the physician's free-text addition elsewhere in the same note documents an actual abnormal finding contradicting the templated normal-exam language. Which is the more reliable source for coding purposes?", options: ["The templated/autotext language should always be trusted over free-text physician additions", "The physician's own specific free-text documentation of the actual abnormal finding is generally more reliable than a generic autotext/template phrase that may not have been individually verified for this specific encounter — a recognized EHR documentation-quality risk (template/autotext over-generation) discussed as part of Domain 5's data-integrity concepts as well", "Both are equally unreliable and neither may be used for coding", "The conflict must always be resolved by deleting the entire note"], correct: 1, explanation: "This is the EHR-era documentation-quality risk (templated/autotext content potentially not individually verified) connecting back to Domain 5's data-integrity discussion — a physician's specific, individualized free-text finding generally carries more weight than a generic template phrase when the two conflict." },
      { stem: "Which best describes the documentation standard needed to support coding a 'hospital-acquired' pressure ulcer (POA = N) as opposed to one present on admission?", options: ["Any pressure ulcer identified at any point during the stay is automatically coded as hospital-acquired regardless of admission-time skin assessment documentation", "A thorough admission-time skin assessment (ideally within the first 24 hours) documenting the absence of the ulcer (or a different/lesser stage) is the key evidence supporting a genuinely hospital-acquired (POA=N) determination — without that admission-time baseline, distinguishing a truly hospital-acquired ulcer from one simply not caught/documented at admission becomes genuinely difficult", "POA status for pressure ulcers is determined solely by the day-of-stay a wound care consult occurs", "Pressure ulcers are exempt from POA reporting entirely"], correct: 1, explanation: "Accurate POA determination for pressure ulcers specifically depends on a documented admission-time skin assessment establishing a baseline — without it, true hospital-acquisition can't be reliably distinguished from a pre-existing ulcer simply not identified/documented until later in the stay, a real-world HAC-program-relevant documentation-integrity concern." },
      { stem: "Which best reflects a documentation-integrity consideration specific to coding a 'catheter-associated urinary tract infection' (CAUTI) as a potential hospital-acquired condition?", options: ["Documentation should support both the UTI diagnosis itself AND its association with an indwelling urinary catheter (including catheter presence/duration), since CAUTI-specific coding and HAC-program identification depend on that specific catheter-association documentation, not just a generic UTI diagnosis", "CAUTI is coded identically to any other UTI with no catheter-related documentation ever needed", "Catheter presence is tracked only by nursing, with zero relevance to coding", "CAUTI can never be identified as a POA=N hospital-acquired condition under any circumstance"], correct: 0, explanation: "Accurately identifying and coding a CAUTI (as distinct from a generic UTI) depends on documentation establishing the catheter-association — directly relevant to both correct diagnosis coding and HAC-program identification, reinforcing the POA/HAC documentation themes from Domain 2/4." },
      { stem: "A discharge summary lists 'discharge medications' including a new anticoagulant, but no progress note anywhere in the chart documents the clinical reasoning/diagnosis prompting the new anticoagulant (e.g., new AFib, new DVT). This represents:", options: ["No documentation concern, since medication lists are self-explanatory", "A potential 'treatment without a stated diagnosis' documentation gap (the same pattern discussed for the insulin/diabetes example) — the new medication suggests an underlying condition that should be documented and coded if clinically supported, warranting further chart review or a query rather than either ignoring the medication or assuming a specific diagnosis", "Grounds to automatically code atrial fibrillation without any further verification", "A billing-only concern with no coding relevance"], correct: 1, explanation: "This is the same 'treatment implies an undocumented diagnosis' gap pattern already established — a new significant medication without a documented clinical indication should prompt further review/query, not assumption of a specific diagnosis or blanket dismissal." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best reflects a documentation-integrity practice for verifying that a coded 'principal procedure' accurately reflects what UHDDS defines as the principal procedure (the procedure performed for definitive treatment, as opposed to one performed for diagnostic or exploratory purposes, when multiple procedures occur during a stay)?", options: ["The first procedure performed chronologically during the stay is always the principal procedure, regardless of its purpose", "Reviewing the operative documentation for ALL procedures performed during the stay to determine which one was performed for definitive treatment of the condition primarily responsible for the admission (the UHDDS principal procedure definition), rather than automatically defaulting to chronological order or the most invasive-sounding procedure", "Whichever procedure has the highest-weighted DRG code is automatically the principal procedure by definition", "Only surgical (not diagnostic) procedures are ever considered when multiple procedures occur"], correct: 1, explanation: "UHDDS's principal procedure definition centers on definitive treatment (as opposed to diagnostic/exploratory purpose) for the condition responsible for the admission — this requires reviewing what each procedure actually accomplished, not defaulting to chronological order, DRG weight, or invasiveness alone." },
      { stem: "A patient's chart shows a physician order for 'strict I&O monitoring, daily weights, fluid restriction' with no documented diagnosis anywhere explaining these orders (which are commonly associated with heart failure or renal impairment management). This scenario illustrates:", options: ["A normal, unremarkable order set requiring no documentation follow-up", "Another example of the 'treatment/monitoring implies an undocumented diagnosis' gap pattern — these specific orders suggest an underlying condition (e.g., heart failure, fluid overload) that should be clinically documented and coded if actually present, warranting further review or query rather than assumption", "Proof the patient definitely has heart failure requiring immediate coding as such", "Something relevant only to nursing care plans, with zero coding relevance"], correct: 1, explanation: "This reinforces the recurring 'clinical orders/monitoring implying an undocumented diagnosis' documentation-gap pattern with a new clinical example (fluid management orders suggesting heart failure/fluid overload) — the correct response is further review/query, not assumption in either direction." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes why a coder should be cautious about relying solely on an EHR's 'problem list' module as the definitive, current source of a patient's active diagnoses for a specific encounter?", options: ["Problem lists are always perfectly maintained and require zero verification", "Problem list modules can become outdated (containing resolved conditions never removed, or missing newly diagnosed conditions not yet added) if not actively maintained by clinical staff, making the actual encounter-specific documentation (notes, orders, results for that visit) a more reliable primary source for what is currently active and relevant to THIS encounter", "Problem lists should never be consulted under any circumstance during coding", "Problem list accuracy is guaranteed by EHR vendor certification requirements with no facility-level maintenance needed"], correct: 1, explanation: "This reinforces the earlier point about static problem-list entries not automatically qualifying for current-encounter reporting — problem lists are a useful reference but require verification against the actual current-encounter documentation, since maintenance quality varies and lists can become stale." },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Domain 3 — Provider Queries",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_NOTE_D3,
    questions: [
      { stem: "A coder identifies a documented echocardiogram finding of 'reduced ejection fraction, systolic dysfunction' with no physician note anywhere connecting this to a heart failure diagnosis. The most appropriate compliant query would:", options: ["Ask the physician to simply confirm 'systolic heart failure' as the diagnosis with only a yes/no option", "Present the objective echo finding and ask the physician to clarify whether this represents an active clinical diagnosis (e.g., systolic heart failure) requiring treatment/management, offering multiple options including 'not clinically significant at this time' or 'other,' allowing independent clinical judgment", "Assume systolic heart failure is present and code it without querying, since the echo finding is objective", "Avoid querying entirely and code only 'abnormal echocardiogram finding' with no further follow-up ever"], correct: 1, explanation: "This is the same non-leading query-construction principle applied to a new clinical scenario — presenting the objective finding and offering genuine clinical options (including 'not clinically significant') rather than asking the physician to simply confirm a predetermined diagnosis." },
      { stem: "A query is constructed asking a physician to clarify whether documented 'acute kidney injury' meets HCC-relevant risk-adjustment criteria for reimbursement optimization purposes, explicitly referencing the reimbursement impact in the query language. This query is problematic primarily because:", options: ["It is fully compliant since referencing reimbursement helps the physician understand why the query matters", "Explicitly framing a query around reimbursement/risk-score optimization (rather than clinical accuracy) risks the query being non-compliant/leading — a query should be framed around obtaining an accurate, clinically supported diagnosis, not around achieving a specific financial or risk-score outcome", "AKI can never be a valid query subject", "Only surgical diagnoses may ever be queried, never medical diagnoses like AKI"], correct: 1, explanation: "Query construction should center on clinical accuracy, not explicitly framing the ask around a financial/reimbursement/risk-score goal — doing so risks both a compliance problem (an implicitly leading query) and the appearance of query practice driven by revenue rather than documentation integrity." },
      { stem: "Which best describes an appropriate approach when a query response conflicts with findings from an objective diagnostic test (e.g., the physician responds 'no acute kidney injury' despite a creatinine trend that appears to meet common AKI criteria)?", options: ["Override the physician's response and code AKI anyway based on the coder's own interpretation of the lab values", "Generally respect the physician's documented clinical judgment as the qualified clinical decision-maker — the physician may have clinical context (e.g., baseline chronic kidney disease, dilutional effects) not apparent from the lab value in isolation — while a facility's CDI/quality program may separately choose to review such patterns in aggregate for education purposes", "Refuse to finalize the encounter's coding indefinitely until the physician changes their answer", "Report the physician to a regulatory body immediately based on a single query response"], correct: 1, explanation: "A physician's clinical judgment, even when it doesn't align with a coder's or a general criteria set's read of an isolated lab value, generally governs — physicians may have clinical context not apparent from a single data point in isolation; this doesn't preclude aggregate pattern review through appropriate CDI/quality channels, but doesn't justify a coder overriding an individual clinical determination." },
      { stem: "A query is drafted asking a physician to clarify the etiology of acute kidney injury, listing options: '(a) prerenal AKI due to hypovolemia, (b) intrinsic AKI (ATN), (c) postrenal AKI due to obstruction, (d) another cause (please specify), (e) not clinically significant/resolved.' This query is best characterized as:", options: ["Leading, because it lists specific etiologies", "Non-leading — it presents multiple genuine, clinically plausible etiology categories plus open-ended and 'not clinically significant' options, allowing the physician to select or specify based on independent clinical judgment rather than being steered toward one predetermined answer", "Non-compliant because AKI etiology should never be queried", "Leading because option (e) exists"], correct: 1, explanation: "A multi-option query listing several genuinely plausible etiologies plus open-ended and negative options is the textbook compliant structure — listing specific plausible options isn't itself leading; steering toward ONE specific predetermined answer is what makes a query leading." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes an appropriate query practice when documentation supports a HCC-relevant chronic condition (e.g., diabetes with complications) that was clearly evaluated at the encounter, but the specific complication type (e.g., which specific neuropathy/nephropathy manifestation) is ambiguous across conflicting notes?", options: ["Skip querying since HCC-relevant conditions should never be queried due to reimbursement sensitivity", "This is a legitimate, clinically-driven query opportunity like any other specificity gap — the HCC-relevance of the condition doesn't change the compliance analysis; the query should still be constructed around the clinical ambiguity itself (which specific complication), non-leading, not around the reimbursement impact", "Automatically code the most severe complication type without querying, since the condition is HCC-relevant", "Query the patient directly instead of the physician"], correct: 1, explanation: "HCC-relevance doesn't change the fundamental query-compliance analysis — a genuine clinical ambiguity (which specific diabetic complication) is a legitimate query opportunity like any other, constructed around the clinical question itself, not the reimbursement stakes." },
      { stem: "A provider is asked, via query, to clarify whether a patient's altered mental status was due to a new acute stroke versus a metabolic cause, and responds by simply writing 'yes' with no further specification. The appropriate next step is:", options: ["Interpret 'yes' as confirming whichever option the coder personally believes is more likely", "Recognize the response as ambiguous/non-responsive to a multi-option query and follow up for genuine clarification (per organizational query-escalation policy), since 'yes' alone doesn't actually answer which specific option was intended", "Code both stroke and metabolic causes automatically since some answer was given", "Discard the query and code nothing, since any response was received"], correct: 1, explanation: "An ambiguous, non-specific response to a multi-option query doesn't actually resolve the clinical question — appropriate follow-up (per the organization's escalation process) is needed rather than the coder guessing which option 'yes' was meant to confirm." },
      { stem: "Which best reflects appropriate query practice when a nurse practitioner (rather than the attending physician) is the most appropriate person to clarify a specific clinical documentation ambiguity for a condition they personally evaluated and are managing?", options: ["Only attending physicians may ever be queried; NPs and other qualified non-physician practitioners can never be query recipients", "A query may generally be directed to whichever qualified healthcare professional is clinically responsible for/knowledgeable about the specific documentation in question — including an NP or other qualified practitioner who personally evaluated the condition — not exclusively the attending physician in every case", "Queries must always go through the hospital's medical director personally, regardless of who documented the finding", "NPs may only receive queries about billing, never clinical documentation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Query recipients should be whoever is clinically responsible for and knowledgeable about the specific documentation at issue — this can appropriately include NPs, PAs, or other qualified practitioners, not exclusively the attending physician in every scenario." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the concept of a 'compliant query template library' maintained by a HIM/CDI department?", options: ["A library is unnecessary since every query must be entirely improvised from scratch each time with no prior review", "A curated set of pre-vetted, non-leading query templates for common clinical scenarios (e.g., AKI etiology, heart failure type, malnutrition severity) that have been reviewed for compliance, used as a starting point that is still tailored to the specific patient's actual clinical indicators for each individual query — not simply auto-populated without case-specific customization", "Query templates automatically make every query compliant regardless of how they are actually used or customized for the specific case", "Templates are prohibited entirely under AHIMA/ACDIS query-practice guidance"], correct: 1, explanation: "A vetted template library is a legitimate compliance tool (ensuring consistent, pre-reviewed non-leading structure), but templates still require case-specific tailoring (the actual patient's clinical indicators) to be genuinely compliant for each individual use — a template alone doesn't guarantee compliance if used without that customization." },
      { stem: "Which best describes an appropriate escalation path when a physician repeatedly fails to respond to compliant, well-constructed queries over an extended pattern (not a single instance)?", options: ["No escalation path should ever exist; coders/CDI staff must simply keep waiting indefinitely", "A pattern of non-response is appropriately escalated through the organization's defined channels (e.g., physician advisor, medical staff leadership, or CDI/HIM department leadership) as a systemic issue, rather than each individual coder handling it in isolation or the pattern going unaddressed", "The correct escalation is to code the most favorable diagnosis automatically after a set number of unanswered queries", "Physicians who don't respond to queries should be reported directly to state licensing boards as the first step"], correct: 1, explanation: "A recurring non-response pattern is a systemic issue appropriately escalated through defined organizational channels — not something addressed by assuming a favorable diagnosis, an immediate licensing-board report as a first step, or leaving each individual coder to handle it alone indefinitely." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best reflects the compliance principle behind NOT querying for a diagnosis solely because it would increase a case's HCC risk score or DRG weight, absent genuine clinical indicators supporting that diagnosis?", options: ["Querying purely to pursue a higher-weighted outcome, without genuine clinical indicators supporting the queried condition, risks constituting 'query fishing' — a compliance concern distinct from a legitimate, indicator-driven query that happens to also affect reimbursement/risk score as a byproduct of accurate coding", "There is no meaningful distinction between querying based on genuine indicators versus querying purely for reimbursement impact", "Reimbursement/risk-score impact should always be the primary factor driving whether to query, more important than genuine clinical indicators", "Only diagnoses that decrease reimbursement may ever be queried, to avoid any appearance of bias"], correct: 0, explanation: "This reinforces the query-fishing concept introduced earlier: the distinguishing factor is whether genuine clinical indicators support the query, not whether the potential answer would affect reimbursement — a query driven by financial outcome rather than actual clinical ambiguity is the compliance risk, regardless of which direction the financial impact runs." },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Domain 4 — Regulatory Compliance",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_NOTE_D4,
    questions: [
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes CMS's general expectation regarding documentation supporting the 'two-midnight' inpatient admission presumption?", options: ["No documentation is needed; the presumption applies automatically to every admission regardless of physician expectation", "The physician's expectation of a stay spanning two midnights, and the medical necessity for hospital-level care, should be reasonably supported by the documented clinical picture at the time of the admission decision — not simply asserted after the fact without contemporaneous clinical support", "Two-midnight documentation requirements apply only to Medicaid patients, never Medicare", "The rule requires exactly two midnights with no flexibility for shorter or longer stays ever being separately justified"], correct: 1, explanation: "The Two-Midnight Rule's presumption is tied to a reasonable, contemporaneously-documented physician expectation and medical necessity — not a rule that can be satisfied by after-the-fact assertion without supporting clinical documentation at the time of the decision." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a compliance risk specifically associated with HCC/risk-adjustment coding practices, as distinguished from inpatient DRG-related compliance risks?", options: ["Risk-adjustment coding carries no unique compliance risks distinct from inpatient DRG coding", "Risk-adjustment-specific risks include diagnosis 'upcoding' or carrying forward HCC-relevant diagnoses without current-encounter clinical support, and CMS/OIG audit activity (including Risk Adjustment Data Validation, RADV, audits) specifically targeting unsupported HCC diagnosis capture in Medicare Advantage and similar programs", "HCC-related compliance risk applies only to outpatient laboratory billing", "RADV audits apply exclusively to inpatient hospital claims, never physician/health-plan risk-adjustment submissions"], correct: 1, explanation: "RADV (Risk Adjustment Data Validation) audits are the HCC/risk-adjustment-specific compliance mechanism (paralleling RAC/CERT for fee-for-service claims) — recognizing this distinct audit/compliance landscape is relevant as risk-adjustment coding knowledge becomes more central to CCS-relevant real-world practice." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the compliance principle behind accurately coding a patient status change from observation to inpatient (or vice versa) during a single hospital stay?", options: ["Status changes require no specific documentation; the final billing status may be selected at the biller's convenience regardless of the clinical/physician determination", "The actual physician-ordered status change (with its own timing and clinical rationale documented) should drive UHDDS/coding treatment of the stay, since inaccurately representing the status change (e.g., billing the entire stay as inpatient when only a later portion was properly converted) misstates both clinical and regulatory reporting", "Status changes are never permitted once a patient is registered under an initial status", "Only the initial admission status matters; any later change during the same stay is coding-irrelevant"], correct: 1, explanation: "An actual, physician-ordered status change during a stay has its own real coding/UHDDS implications — accurately reflecting when and why that change occurred (rather than either ignoring it or applying a status to the whole stay retroactively without basis) is a genuine regulatory-compliance-relevant documentation and coding concept." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a facility's obligation regarding coding compliance auditing specifically for HCC-relevant diagnoses submitted for Medicare Advantage risk adjustment?", options: ["No special auditing obligation exists beyond general DRG-focused compliance auditing", "Given RADV audit exposure and the direct payment impact of HCC diagnoses, facilities/health plans generally should include HCC-specific accuracy auditing (verifying clinical support and current-encounter relevance for submitted HCC diagnoses) as part of a comprehensive compliance program, not relying solely on general inpatient-DRG-focused audit activities", "HCC auditing is solely the health plan's responsibility with zero facility/provider-side compliance role", "HCC-relevant diagnoses are self-auditing and require no human review process at all"], correct: 1, explanation: "Given RADV exposure and HCC's direct financial impact, a comprehensive compliance program should specifically include risk-adjustment-focused auditing — not assume general DRG-focused compliance activities alone adequately cover this distinct risk area." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a Risk Adjustment Data Validation (RADV) audit?", options: ["A CMS audit process (and, at the plan level, an OIG/CMS-driven process) verifying that HCC diagnosis codes submitted for risk-adjusted payment (e.g., Medicare Advantage) are actually supported by medical record documentation, with unsupported diagnoses potentially resulting in payment recoupment", "A one-time audit that occurred only during the initial rollout of ICD-10-CM and is no longer performed", "An audit exclusively of inpatient DRG assignment, unrelated to any risk-adjustment methodology", "A voluntary audit facilities may choose to skip entirely with no consequence"], correct: 0, explanation: "RADV audits are the specific validation mechanism for risk-adjustment/HCC diagnosis accuracy, distinct from inpatient DRG-focused audits like RAC/CERT — unsupported HCC diagnoses found during RADV can result in payment recoupment, making documentation/coding accuracy for these diagnoses a real, ongoing compliance exposure area." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes why 'diagnosis dropping' (a chronic HCC-relevant condition present in a prior year's risk score simply not being re-addressed/re-coded in the current year) is generally NOT itself considered improper, as distinguished from affirmatively coding an unsupported diagnosis?", options: ["Diagnosis dropping and affirmatively coding an unsupported diagnosis are equally improper and treated identically under compliance standards", "A condition genuinely not addressed/evaluated at any encounter during the current year appropriately doesn't carry forward into that year's risk score under the annual-recapture design of risk-adjustment models — this is different from affirmatively documenting/coding a diagnosis that ISN'T actually supported by current clinical evidence, which IS a compliance concern", "Diagnosis dropping is illegal and must always be affirmatively corrected by coding the condition regardless of whether it was addressed that year", "Risk-adjustment models have no annual component; all HCCs are permanent regardless of any given year's documentation"], correct: 1, explanation: "This distinguishes two different scenarios: a condition genuinely not addressed in the current year appropriately not carrying forward (a designed feature of annual risk-adjustment models, not a compliance violation) versus affirmatively coding an unsupported diagnosis (a genuine compliance risk) — conflating the two is a common misunderstanding." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the compliance concept of 'chart chasing' or 'retrospective chart review' in the risk-adjustment coding context, and its appropriate limits?", options: ["Retrospective review of medical records to identify previously undocumented/uncoded HCC-relevant diagnoses that WERE genuinely addressed/supported during a prior encounter is a legitimate compliance-supportive practice, but affirmatively adding diagnoses not actually supported by the documentation of that specific encounter would not be appropriate", "Retrospective chart review is always improper regardless of what it finds", "Chart review may add any diagnosis a reviewer believes is clinically likely, even without documentation support", "Chart review applies only to future encounters, never past ones"], correct: 0, explanation: "Legitimate retrospective review identifies genuinely supported-but-uncoded diagnoses from an already-documented encounter — it is not a license to add diagnoses unsupported by that encounter's actual documentation, which would cross into the same unsupported-diagnosis compliance risk already discussed." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the compliance relevance of a 'query volume/outcome' pattern where a specific CDI specialist's queries result in a diagnosis being added in nearly 100% of cases, specifically for HCC-relevant or CC/MCC-relevant conditions?", options: ["This pattern has no compliance relevance and should never be reviewed", "Such a skewed pattern is a recognized internal-monitoring flag potentially indicating leading query construction or query-fishing, warranting review of that specialist's actual query language/practice — not an automatic presumption of wrongdoing, but a legitimate signal worth investigating", "A 100% agreement rate is always proof of excellent CDI work with no further review warranted", "Only physician response patterns matter; CDI specialist query construction is never itself subject to compliance review"], correct: 1, explanation: "An unusually skewed outcome pattern (near-universal agreement, especially on financially significant queries) is a recognized internal compliance-monitoring signal warranting review of the underlying query construction — not an automatic conclusion of wrongdoing, but a legitimate basis for further investigation, reinforcing the earlier query-metrics-monitoring concept." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a compliant approach to addressing an observation-to-inpatient status conversion that occurs LATE in a stay (e.g., on day 3 of what began as observation), specifically regarding the physician documentation needed to support it?", options: ["No specific documentation is needed; status may be changed retroactively in the billing system alone without any updated physician order/rationale", "The conversion should be supported by a physician order (or equivalent) reflecting the updated clinical assessment/rationale for inpatient-level care at the time of the actual determination, since UHDDS/coding treatment of the stay should reflect the genuine clinical decision-making timeline, not simply a retroactive billing system change unsupported by contemporaneous physician documentation", "Status conversions occurring after the first 24 hours are never permitted under any payer's policy", "Only the admitting physician (never a covering or attending physician) may ever order a status conversion"], correct: 1, explanation: "A status conversion should be supported by contemporaneous physician documentation reflecting the actual clinical decision at the time it was made — a billing-system-only change unsupported by a physician's own documented rationale would misrepresent the genuine clinical decision-making timeline and create compliance exposure." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the compliance concept behind CMS's 'condition code 44' process?", options: ["A billing code used only for ambulance transport", "A specific process allowing a hospital to change a patient's status from inpatient to outpatient/observation when, upon internal utilization review BEFORE discharge, it's determined the inpatient admission didn't meet medical necessity criteria — governed by specific procedural requirements (physician concurrence, UR committee involvement, before discharge)", "A code indicating a patient left against medical advice", "A modifier used exclusively in the CPT Surgery section"], correct: 1, explanation: "Condition code 44 is the specific, procedurally-defined mechanism for a compliant inpatient-to-outpatient status change following internal utilization review before discharge — a real regulatory-compliance process directly connected to the medical-necessity/Two-Midnight Rule concepts discussed earlier." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes why 'self-disclosure' to OIG/CMS of an identified overpayment or compliance issue is sometimes considered a favorable compliance practice, despite disclosing a problem?", options: ["Self-disclosure guarantees complete immunity from any consequences whatsoever", "Voluntary, proactive self-disclosure (through CMS/OIG's self-disclosure protocols) is generally viewed more favorably than a problem later discovered through external audit, and can result in more favorable resolution terms — reflecting the broader compliance-program principle that proactive identification/correction is preferable to concealment or inaction", "Self-disclosure is legally prohibited and facilities should never do it under any circumstance", "Self-disclosure applies only to billing errors under $100, with no relevance to larger issues"], correct: 1, explanation: "Voluntary self-disclosure protocols exist specifically because proactive correction is treated more favorably than issues surfaced through external audit — reinforcing the broader compliance principle (from the 60-day overpayment rule and corrective-action discussions) that proactive identification and correction is the preferred compliance posture." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the general compliance principle behind maintaining detailed documentation of a coding compliance program's ongoing training, auditing, and corrective-action activities (i.e., documenting the compliance program's own operation)?", options: ["Documenting the compliance program's own activities is unnecessary busywork with no practical value", "Maintaining records of the compliance program's actual training, auditing, and corrective-action activities provides evidence the program is genuinely operating (not merely existing on paper) — relevant both to internal effectiveness assessment and to demonstrating good-faith compliance efforts if the organization is ever subject to external review", "Only the initial compliance policy document needs to exist; ongoing activity records serve no purpose", "Compliance program documentation is relevant only to for-profit hospitals, never nonprofit or government facilities"], correct: 1, explanation: "A compliance program's credibility (both internally and to external reviewers) depends on evidence it's actually operating — ongoing records of real training/auditing/corrective-action activity, not just a policy document that exists on paper without demonstrated implementation." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason for a facility to track and trend its own HAC/PSI rates over time, beyond the direct payment impact?", options: ["Trending has no value beyond the immediate payment calculation for the current reporting period", "Trending HAC/PSI rates over time can help identify genuine clinical quality/safety improvement opportunities (distinct from pure documentation/coding accuracy issues) as well as surface potential documentation or POA-assignment accuracy issues if a facility's coded rates diverge significantly from its actual clinical experience", "HAC/PSI rates are static, single-point-in-time measures that cannot be meaningfully trended", "Only external auditors, never the facility itself, have any legitimate reason to review HAC/PSI trends"], correct: 1, explanation: "Trending HAC/PSI data serves both a genuine clinical-quality purpose and a documentation/coding-accuracy verification purpose — a facility whose coded rates diverge significantly from its actual clinical experience should investigate whether that reflects genuine quality differences, or POA/documentation accuracy issues, or both." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the compliance principle behind ensuring coding staff productivity metrics do not implicitly penalize time spent on legitimate, necessary provider queries?", options: ["Productivity metrics should always be structured to discourage any query activity, since queries slow down coding throughput", "A well-designed compliance-supportive coding department should structure productivity expectations in a way that doesn't create a perverse incentive to skip legitimate, necessary queries purely to hit a raw case-per-hour target — since discouraging appropriate query practice through productivity pressure undermines documentation/coding accuracy", "Productivity metrics and query practice are entirely unrelated with no interaction ever considered in compliance program design", "Only individual coders, never department leadership, bear responsibility for this balance"], correct: 1, explanation: "This reinforces the earlier productivity-pressure-vs-accuracy tension, applied specifically to query practice: department-level metric design that implicitly discourages necessary queries (by only rewarding raw speed) is itself a compliance-program design concern, not just an individual coder's ethical challenge." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason a facility might conduct a focused audit specifically on POA indicator accuracy for a sample of pressure ulcer and CAUTI/CLABSI diagnoses?", options: ["POA accuracy for these specific HAC-relevant condition types has direct payment and quality-reporting impact, making a focused audit a reasonable, targeted compliance activity rather than relying solely on general, unfocused coding audits to catch this specific risk area", "POA indicators for these conditions are always 100% accurate by default and require no auditing", "Focused audits are prohibited; only fully comprehensive, all-diagnosis audits are permitted under compliance program standards", "POA accuracy has no connection to HAC-program payment consequences"], correct: 0, explanation: "Given the specific, direct HAC-program payment/reporting consequences tied to accurate POA assignment for these particular condition types, a targeted/focused audit approach is a reasonable, efficient way to specifically verify accuracy in a known higher-risk area — rather than relying only on general audits to incidentally catch this risk." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the compliance principle behind a facility having a clearly defined policy on which staff roles are authorized to finalize/submit a claim after coding is complete (i.e., separation between coding and claim-submission authorization)?", options: ["No such distinction or policy is necessary; anyone with system access should be able to both code and submit claims interchangeably", "Clearly defining and separating who may code versus who may authorize final claim submission supports internal control and accountability — helping ensure a claim isn't submitted with codes that haven't gone through the organization's actual coding/QA process", "This separation-of-duties concept applies only to financial/accounting departments, never to HIM/coding/billing workflows", "Claim submission authorization is solely an IT system permission setting with no compliance-policy relevance"], correct: 1, explanation: "This extends the separation-of-duties concept (already introduced regarding coding vs. reimbursement-negotiation functions) to the coding-to-claim-submission workflow specifically — a defined authorization policy supports accountability and helps ensure claims reflect properly QA'd coding, not a bypass of that process." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes why understanding both RAC/CERT (fee-for-service focused) AND RADV (risk-adjustment focused) audit programs matters for a well-rounded CCS-credentialed coder working across both inpatient/outpatient fee-for-service and value-based/Medicare Advantage coding contexts?", options: ["Only one of the two audit frameworks is ever relevant to any single coder's actual work, so understanding both is unnecessary", "As healthcare payment increasingly spans both traditional fee-for-service (RAC/CERT-relevant) and risk-adjusted/value-based models (RADV-relevant), a coder working across these contexts benefits from understanding both audit frameworks' distinct focus areas and documentation-support requirements, rather than assuming a single compliance framework covers all payment/audit exposure", "RAC, CERT, and RADV are simply three different names for the exact same audit process", "Compliance audit programs are entirely payer-internal matters with zero relevance to coding staff regardless of context"], correct: 1, explanation: "Given the real-world shift toward risk-adjusted/value-based payment alongside continuing fee-for-service volume, a well-rounded coder benefits from recognizing that RAC/CERT and RADV are DISTINCT audit frameworks with different focus areas (code-pair/DRG accuracy vs. HCC diagnosis support) — not interchangeable or covered by a single compliance lens." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason to periodically reconcile a sample of a facility's submitted HCC diagnoses against the actual supporting clinical documentation, independent of any external RADV audit request?", options: ["Internal reconciliation has no value since only external RADV findings matter for compliance purposes", "Proactive internal reconciliation (essentially a facility's own mini-RADV-style self-check) helps identify and correct unsupported HCC diagnoses BEFORE an external audit finds them, consistent with the broader self-disclosure/proactive-correction compliance principle already discussed", "Internal reconciliation is only appropriate after a facility has already failed an external RADV audit", "HCC diagnosis reconciliation is solely a health plan responsibility, with zero facility-side role appropriate"], correct: 1, explanation: "This applies the broader proactive-compliance-monitoring principle specifically to HCC/RADV exposure — a facility-level self-check before external audit exposure is a natural extension of the self-disclosure/proactive-correction philosophy already established for general compliance auditing." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the compliance concept behind ensuring a facility's EHR/CAC system configuration doesn't automatically default a POA indicator to 'Y' for every diagnosis without genuine case-by-case clinical determination?", options: ["A system default is always acceptable and requires no oversight regardless of the actual clinical facts of each case", "System configurations that apply a blanket default POA value without requiring/prompting an actual case-specific clinical determination risk systematically misstating POA data (in either direction) at scale — a system-design compliance risk distinct from, but related to, the individual-case documentation-integrity concerns already discussed for POA accuracy", "POA indicators are never affected by system configuration, only by manual coder entry", "System defaults for POA are required by CMS and cannot be questioned or reviewed by facility compliance staff"], correct: 1, explanation: "A system-level default that bypasses genuine case-by-case clinical determination is a system-design compliance risk operating 'at scale' — distinct from, but related to, the individual documentation-review concerns already covered, and a legitimate thing for compliance/HIM leadership to review in system configuration, not just individual coder behavior." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the compliance rationale for requiring documented physician concurrence (not just a UR nurse's determination alone) before a condition code 44 status change from inpatient to outpatient/observation may be finalized?", options: ["Physician concurrence is not actually required for condition code 44; a UR nurse's determination alone is always sufficient", "CMS's condition code 44 process specifically requires the physician's agreement with the utilization review determination (documented before discharge), since a clinical status determination like this appropriately requires physician-level clinical judgment, not solely a nursing/UR administrative determination", "Physician concurrence is only needed for outpatient-to-inpatient conversions, never the reverse direction", "Condition code 44 applies only to Medicaid patients, never Medicare"], correct: 1, explanation: "Condition code 44's procedural requirements specifically include physician concurrence with the UR determination — reflecting that this is a clinical status determination requiring physician-level judgment, not a purely administrative/nursing UR decision made in isolation." },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Domain 5 — Information Technologies",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_NOTE_D5,
    questions: [
      { stem: "HL7 (Health Level Seven) is best described as:", options: ["A single software product sold by one specific vendor", "A set of international standards for the exchange, integration, sharing, and retrieval of electronic health information, providing a common framework/language different systems can use to communicate", "A government regulatory agency that licenses hospitals", "A billing code set that replaced CPT"], correct: 1, explanation: "HL7 is a standards-development organization/family of interoperability standards (not a single vendor product or a code set) — it defines how health information systems structure and exchange data with each other." },
      { stem: "FHIR (Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources) is best described as:", options: ["An older, now-deprecated HL7 standard no longer in active use", "A modern HL7 interoperability standard built on web-based technologies (using discrete 'resources' representing clinical concepts like Patient, Observation, Condition) designed to make health data exchange more developer-friendly and API-based than some older HL7 messaging standards", "A proprietary standard owned exclusively by a single EHR vendor", "A coding classification system that replaces ICD-10-CM"], correct: 1, explanation: "FHIR is HL7's more modern, actively evolving standard — built around discrete, web/API-friendly 'resources' — distinguishing it from older HL7 messaging standards (like HL7 v2) while still being part of the broader HL7 family, not a replacement for ICD-10-CM or a single-vendor product." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes why interoperability standards like HL7/FHIR are relevant to HIM/coding practice specifically, beyond pure IT infrastructure concerns?", options: ["They have no relevance to HIM/coding whatsoever, being purely an IT department concern", "As health information increasingly flows between organizations via these standards, HIM/coding professionals need to understand both the opportunities (more complete external clinical data available for documentation/coding context) and the data-quality verification responsibilities (as discussed in Domain 2) that come with using externally-sourced, standards-exchanged data", "Interoperability standards eliminate the need for any human coding or HIM review entirely", "HL7/FHIR apply only to laboratory data, never clinical documentation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Interoperability standards directly affect what clinical information HIM/coding staff encounter (data flowing in from other organizations) and carry their own data-quality verification considerations — a genuine cross-domain connection between Domain 5 (IT) and Domain 2 (documentation integrity) content." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a basic cybersecurity concept relevant to HIM/coding operations, specifically 'phishing,' as distinguished from a technical system vulnerability?", options: ["Phishing is a hardware failure requiring IT repair, unrelated to human behavior", "Phishing is a social-engineering attack technique (e.g., a deceptive email attempting to trick a staff member into revealing credentials or clicking a malicious link) that exploits human behavior rather than a purely technical system flaw — making workforce security awareness training (an administrative safeguard under the Security Rule) a relevant countermeasure alongside technical controls", "Phishing applies only to personal email accounts, never workplace systems", "Phishing has no connection to HIPAA Security Rule administrative safeguards"], correct: 1, explanation: "Phishing specifically targets human behavior rather than exploiting a pure technical flaw, which is exactly why workforce training (an administrative safeguard) is a core, relevant Security Rule countermeasure alongside technical controls like spam filtering — connecting Domain 5's cybersecurity awareness to Domain 4's HIPAA Security Rule administrative safeguard concept." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes 'ransomware,' as relevant to HIM/coding system availability and Security Rule compliance considerations?", options: ["A type of beneficial software that improves system performance", "Malicious software that encrypts an organization's data/systems, rendering them inaccessible until a ransom is paid (or the organization recovers via backups), representing both a security incident and a potential HIPAA breach/availability concern directly relevant to Security Rule contingency planning (backup and disaster recovery) requirements", "A billing term for delayed insurance reimbursement", "A type of EHR software update"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ransomware attacks are a real, significant healthcare-sector cybersecurity threat directly implicating Security Rule contingency-planning requirements (data backup, disaster recovery, emergency mode operations) — a practical connection between cybersecurity awareness and the administrative/technical safeguard planning obligations already covered under Domain 4/5 content." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the concept of 'access controls' (e.g., role-based access) as a HIPAA Security Rule technical safeguard relevant to HIM/coding system design?", options: ["All staff, regardless of job role, should have identical, unrestricted access to every system and every patient record for efficiency", "Role-based access control assigns system/data access permissions based on a staff member's specific job function/need (e.g., coders accessing coding-relevant systems and records, without unrestricted access to unrelated departments' data), operationalizing the 'minimum necessary' principle at the technical/system level", "Access controls apply only to physicians, never to HIM/coding staff", "Role-based access is a marketing term with no actual technical implementation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Role-based access control is the technical-safeguard mechanism that operationalizes the 'minimum necessary' principle discussed under Domain 4 — assigning system access aligned to actual job-function need rather than blanket unrestricted access, a concrete connection between Domain 4 compliance principles and Domain 5 technical implementation." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes 'multi-factor authentication' (MFA) as a HIPAA Security Rule technical safeguard relevant to HIM/coding system access?", options: ["A system requiring only a username, with no password ever needed", "Requiring two or more independent verification factors (e.g., a password plus a one-time code sent to a registered device) before granting access to a system containing ePHI, reducing the risk that a single compromised credential (e.g., a phished password) alone grants unauthorized access", "A feature that automatically shares login credentials across all staff to simplify access", "A safeguard that applies only to patient-facing portals, never to internal HIM/coding staff systems"], correct: 1, explanation: "MFA specifically addresses the risk that a single compromised factor (like a phished password) alone is enough for unauthorized access — directly relevant to protecting coding/HIM systems that access ePHI, and a natural technical countermeasure connecting back to the earlier phishing discussion." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes 'encryption at rest' versus 'encryption in transit,' both relevant to protecting ePHI accessed by HIM/coding systems?", options: ["They are the same concept applied at different times of day", "Encryption at rest protects data stored on a device/server/database when not actively being transmitted, while encryption in transit protects data as it moves across a network (e.g., between a coder's workstation and a remote server) — both are relevant technical safeguards addressing different points of potential exposure", "Only encryption in transit is ever required by the Security Rule; encryption at rest is never relevant", "Encryption at rest applies only to paper records"], correct: 1, explanation: "At-rest and in-transit encryption address different exposure points (stored data vs. data actively moving across a network) — both are relevant technical safeguard considerations for systems (including remote/telework coding setups) that access ePHI." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a 'business associate agreement' (BAA) as it relates to a third-party CAC/encoder software vendor with access to a facility's ePHI?", options: ["BAAs are unnecessary for software vendors, only for other healthcare providers", "A BAA is a required HIPAA contract establishing a vendor's obligations to appropriately safeguard ePHI it accesses/processes on the covered entity's behalf — a third-party encoder/CAC/interoperability vendor with ePHI access generally requires one", "BAAs apply only to billing companies, never to coding-support software vendors", "A BAA replaces the need for the vendor to comply with the HIPAA Security Rule at all"], correct: 1, explanation: "Any vendor accessing/processing ePHI on a covered entity's behalf (including CAC/encoder software vendors) generally requires a business associate agreement establishing their own HIPAA safeguard obligations — a practical Domain 5 vendor-management concept connecting technology adoption to compliance obligations." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes a reasonable HIM/IT governance consideration when adopting a cloud-based (rather than on-premises) encoder, CAC, or abstracting platform?", options: ["Cloud-based systems require no different compliance consideration than on-premises systems, since the Security Rule doesn't distinguish deployment models", "Cloud deployment introduces additional considerations (vendor BAA obligations, data location/residency, vendor security posture, access control configuration) that should be evaluated as part of the organization's risk analysis and vendor management process, alongside the same core Security Rule safeguard requirements that apply regardless of deployment model", "Cloud-based systems are prohibited under HIPAA for any ePHI-touching HIM function", "Only on-premises systems can ever be HIPAA-compliant"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cloud deployment doesn't change the underlying Security Rule obligations, but it does introduce additional practical considerations (vendor BAA, data residency, vendor security posture) that a thorough risk analysis and vendor-management process should specifically address — a real, increasingly common HIM/IT governance scenario." },
    ],
  },
];
